  <p>
    MC2R is the odd one out of the melanocortin family. Where the other four
    receptors respond to several melanocortin peptides, MC2R answers to just one —
    ACTH — and it sits in just one main place: the cortex of the adrenal gland,
    where it forms the final step of the body's stress-hormone axis.<Cite n={1} />
    It is also the only one of the five that is not a complete machine on its
    own. What follows is the story of a receptor that needs a partner, reads a
    password, and was the first in the family to explain a human disease.
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="A two-component receptor. Alone, MC2R is stranded inside the cell; MRAP1 escorts it to the surface and reshapes it to read ACTH. The ligand must carry both the shared His-Phe-Arg-Trp message and a basic address the MSH peptides lack — which is why only ACTH makes cortisol."
    source="Schematic, after Metherell et al. 2005, Sebag & Hinkle 2007, Dores 2009 and Luo et al. 2023."
  >
    <TwoComponentReceptor />
  </Figure>

  <h2>The end of the stress axis</h2>
  <p>
    Stress and circadian cues drive the hypothalamus to release CRH, which makes
    the pituitary secrete ACTH, which travels in the blood to the adrenal cortex
    and binds MC2R. Like its relatives, MC2R couples to Gs and raises cyclic AMP;
    through protein kinase A this switches on the steroidogenic machinery that
    produces cortisol (and contributes to adrenal androgens).<Cite n={2} /> This
    hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal (HPA) loop is the circuit that the{' '}
    <a href="/system">overview pillar</a> describes <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>{' '}
    serving — and MC2R is its receiving end. The receptor is expressed in all three
    zones of the adrenal cortex, most densely in the cortisol-producing zona
    fasciculata, and ACTH acting through it is trophic as well as acute: chronic
    ACTH excess makes the cortex grow, and ACTH deficiency makes it
    shrink.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>The MC2R gene</h2>
  <p>
    MC2R was cloned in 1992 alongside MC1R, in the paper that founded the
    melanocortin receptor family. The screen was designed to find the receptor for
    MSH in melanoma cells; the second gene it pulled out was expressed instead in
    the adrenal cortex and encoded the long-sought ACTH receptor.<Cite n={4} /> It
    is the smallest G-protein-coupled receptor in the family — 297 amino acids,
    against 332 for <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> — and, like all five, it is
    encoded by a single intronless exon. The gene sits on the short arm of
    chromosome 18, at 18p11.21, immediately adjacent to{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc5r">MC5R</a>; the two are thought to have arisen by a
    local duplication.<Cite n={2} /> It was also the first melanocortin receptor
    to be tied to a human disease. Within a year of its cloning, a point mutation
    in MC2R was found in a family with inherited adrenal failure, which made the
    ACTH receptor one of the earliest GPCRs linked to a Mendelian
    disorder.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>

  <h2>Why only ACTH?</h2>
  <p>
    The melanocortins share a common His-Phe-Arg-Trp core, which is enough to
    activate MC1R and MC3R–MC5R. MC2R is different: it additionally requires a
    stretch of ACTH present only in the full hormone and not in the shorter MSH
    peptides.<Cite n={1} /> The idea goes back to Robert Schwyzer, who proposed
    in the 1970s that ACTH carries two kinds of information: a “message” — the
    His-Phe-Arg-Trp motif at positions 6–9 that is common to the whole family —
    and an “address”, the run of basic residues Lys-Lys-Arg-Arg-Pro at positions
    15–19 that the adrenal receptor demands.<Cite n={9} /> α-MSH is ACTH(1–13):
    it has the message but stops before the address, and so cannot meaningfully
    switch MC2R on. Mutagenesis and chimera studies have since located the
    receptor residues that read the address and shown how MRAP1 helps present
    them, though the picture is still being refined.<Cite n={10} /> The practical
    consequence is a built-in guard: a system that uses the same core peptide to
    colour skin, curb appetite and resolve inflammation can do all of that
    without accidentally triggering cortisol. It also sets the minimum size of any
    MC2R agonist. The shortest ACTH fragment with full adrenal activity is
    ACTH(1–24), which is exactly what the clinical test compound is.
  </p>

  <h2>It needs a chaperone: MRAP</h2>
  <p>
    On its own, MC2R cannot reach the cell surface. For years this made it almost
    impossible to study, because the receptor refused to work in any cell line
    that was not adrenal. The explanation came in 2005 from human genetics. In
    families with inherited adrenal failure but no MC2R mutation, the culprit
    turned out to be a previously unnamed gene encoding a small single-pass
    membrane protein — the melanocortin-2-receptor accessory protein, MRAP —
    that interacts with MC2R and is required for it to leave the endoplasmic
    reticulum and respond to ACTH.<Cite n={6} /> Expressing MRAP alongside MC2R
    finally let the receptor function in ordinary cells, and reframed MC2R as a
    two-component system: receptor plus escort.
  </p>
  <p>
    MRAP is odd in its own right. It inserts into the membrane in both
    orientations and pairs up as an antiparallel homodimer — one copy with its
    amino-terminus outside the cell, the other with it inside — a topology that
    had no precedent among membrane proteins.<Cite n={7} /> The amino-terminal
    region, which carries a short motif essential for ACTH binding, therefore
    appears on both sides of the membrane at once. Its paralogue MRAP2 can also
    carry MC2R to the surface but, unlike MRAP1, does not support a normal ACTH
    response; in the lab it competes with MRAP1 and dampens MC2R signalling.
    Both proteins also interact with the other four melanocortin receptors,
    where MRAP1 and MRAP2 tend to reduce surface expression and
    signalling.<Cite n={8} /> That is the origin of a distinction this site keeps
    stressing: MRAP1 is MC2R's indispensable partner; MRAP2 is a modulator, best
    known for tuning <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc3r">MC3R</a>, and its variants are linked to obesity
    rather than to adrenal disease.
  </p>

  <h2>The receptor in three dimensions</h2>
  <p>
    MC2R was among the last of the five to be seen, and it could not be seen
    alone. The 2023 cryo-EM structure captured the human receptor in complex with
    MRAP1, bound to ACTH and coupled to Gs.<Cite n={11} /> The chaperone wraps
    across the receptor's transmembrane bundle and extracellular face, and the
    structure explains both of MC2R's peculiarities at once. MRAP1 stabilises the
    receptor fold, which is why MC2R cannot traffic without it, and it reshapes
    the entrance to the binding pocket so that a hormone far larger than the
    His-Phe-Arg-Trp core can be read — including the basic address that no MSH
    peptide carries.<Cite n={11} /> The core itself sits in the family's shared
    calcium-assisted pocket, which is why drugs aimed at MC4R or MC1R can brush
    MC2R, and why selectivity is the melanocortin field's recurring problem. The{' '}
    <a href="/structures">structural biology</a> page tells that story for the
    whole family.
  </p>

  <h2>When it fails: familial glucocorticoid deficiency</h2>
  <p>
    Loss-of-function mutations in MC2R cause familial glucocorticoid deficiency
    (FGD) type 1, an autosomal recessive condition in which the adrenal cortex
    cannot mount a normal cortisol response to ACTH. Children present in infancy
    or early childhood with hypoglycaemia, seizures, recurrent infection, failure
    to thrive, and often deep skin pigmentation — because the pituitary, sensing
    no cortisol, pours out ACTH, and the excess spills over onto the pigment
    receptor <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>. Mineralocorticoid production is
    characteristically preserved, since aldosterone answers mainly to the
    renin–angiotensin system rather than to ACTH.<Cite n={2} /> When the
    disease-causing mutations were examined one by one, most turned out to work
    not by abolishing signalling but by stranding the receptor inside the cell —
    even when MRAP was present — a trafficking failure rather than a signalling
    one.<Cite n={3} /> MRAP mutations cause the clinically similar FGD type 2,
    which tends to present even earlier; together the two genes account for
    roughly half of FGD, and the remainder has since been traced to genes for
    oxidative-stress defence and DNA repair in the adrenal.<Cite n={2} /> It is a
    reminder that, here, the chaperone is as load-bearing as the receptor.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the adrenal</h2>
  <p>
    MC2R is close to adrenal-specific in humans, but not perfectly so. In rodents
    it is clearly expressed in fat, where ACTH is a potent lipolytic hormone, and
    MC2R messenger RNA is found in both white and brown adipose depots and rises
    sharply as fat cells differentiate.<Cite n={13} /> That makes ACTH a direct
    mobiliser of stored energy during stress in mice — a peripheral melanocortin
    effect to set beside the fuel-handling role of <a href="/receptors/mc5r">MC5R</a>.
    In humans the adipose expression is low and the lipolytic effect of ACTH
    modest, and the receptor has also been reported at low levels in
    skin.<Cite n={2} /> The
    clinically important lesson runs the other way: because MC2R is so nearly
    confined to the adrenal, a drug that hits it will have, in effect, one
    organ-level consequence, and that consequence is cortisol.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters clinically</h2>
  <p>
    MC2R is the molecular target of the standard test of adrenal reserve. The
    short Synacthen or cosyntropin test gives a dose of synthetic ACTH(1–24) —
    tetracosactide — and measures whether the adrenal cortex, through MC2R,
    answers with a rise in cortisol; a blunted response is the recommended
    confirmatory test for primary adrenal insufficiency.<Cite n={12} /> The same
    peptide, as a depot preparation, is used therapeutically in a handful of
    conditions, which the <a href="/therapeutics">therapeutics</a> page covers.
    The axis also explains a classic clinical sign: disorders that drive ACTH very
    high, from Addison's disease to FGD, darken the skin because the excess ACTH
    reaches <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a>. It is the melanocortin system
    quietly showing its unity — one family, read in two very different tissues.
  </p>

  <h2>Blocking the ACTH receptor</h2>
  <p>
    The newest chapter is the attempt to turn MC2R off. Several conditions are
    driven by too much ACTH signalling at the adrenal: congenital adrenal
    hyperplasia, where a cortisol-synthesis block sends ACTH soaring and the
    excess drives adrenal androgen overproduction; and ACTH-dependent Cushing's
    syndrome, from pituitary or ectopic tumours that secrete the hormone. A
    selective MC2R antagonist would cut the adrenal response at its receptor
    whatever the source of the ACTH, and oral non-peptide antagonists have now
    reached mid-stage clinical trials in both conditions. In a 12-week phase 2
    study in adults with classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia, the first of
    them, atumelnant, cut the androgen precursor androstenedione by well over half
    within days and held it there, on top of unchanged glucocorticoid
    replacement.<Cite n={14} /> None has yet been approved,
    and the obvious design constraint is that the brake must be adjustable: a
    patient still needs cortisol, so an antagonist has to be dosed, or paired with
    replacement, rather than simply switched on. For the state of play across
    the family, see the <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pharmacologically MC2R is the cleanest target in the family, because its
    strict ligand requirement means an antagonist built around the ACTH address
    need not touch the four receptors that read only the message. The problem the
    other receptors face — the shared pocket that makes a{' '}
    <a href="/mc1r-selectivity">clean MC1R drug</a> so hard — is, at MC2R,
    solved by the biology itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    For how MC2R fits the broader system, see the{' '}
    <a href="/system">overview pillar</a>; its only ligand has its own page at{' '}
    <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>.
  </p>

